red set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Administrative Level\n"Variable description":  levels.<br>1. King<br>when the Zuwa dynasty was replaced in the mid-15th Century, the Songhay kings had the title "sii (short for sonyi)" <br>kings called dias until c1335 CE. After that titles were sunni or shi.<br>__Court Bureaucracy__<br>Askia Muhammad Toure "supported by Mande clans ... created a standing army and a central bureaucracy."<br>"The Songhay empire, like that of Mali before it thus involved a gigantic effort of state enterprise in production and trade as well as in military operations and civil government."<br>2. barey-koyin charge of court-arrangements<br>3. kukura-koyassistant, "whose job it was to provide food and other necessary supplies"<br>3. garei-farmerassistant, called master of the camp.<br>2. katisi-farmahead of finance.<br>3. waney-farmaassistant, "responsible for questions of property".<br>3. bara-farmaassistant, in charge of wages.<br>3. dey-farmaassistant, buying and selling activities of government.<br>2. fari-mudiafarming official<br>2. sao-farmaforestry official<br>2. asari-mundiahead of the department of justice<br>Slave colonies<br>"the Songhay empire depended greatly on its colonies of royal slaves and on its privileged castes of craftsmen, which had probably been built up originally from the more skilled groups of war captives, such as smiths, weavers and leather-workers. Here again, Songhay took over a system already initiated in Mali, while adding greatly to the numbers of slaves by means of the regular annual raids carried out by the Songhay cavalry among the unprotected, stateless peoples living south of the Niger bend." 3. State farms manager (possibly 4. if responsible to an official from the court)State farms "were spread right across the empire, to supply the government and the garrisons, but the largest concentration was still to be found in the well-watered inland delta" - a lot of this grain went to the towns, desert caravans and salt mines. <br>4. Lower-level official<br>__Regional government__<br>2. Western Songhai (Mande speakers) - rule based in Timbuktu?"Not under Muhammad only, but also under the succession of sons and grandsons who followed him as Akiyas until 1591, the real thrust of Songhay was toward the west and the north. It was an impetus based upon Timbuktu, both as the centre of Islamic learning in the western Sudan and as the meeting-point of river and desert communications.<br>Askia Muhammad divided the army into two parts: "one for the western provinces based in Timbuktu and one for the eastern provinces based in Gao." 3. Provinces"Under Askia Muhammad, the Songhai Empire established lands in which the kings paid tribute." 4. Town - kafu ruled by a famaWithin the Mande-speaking heartland the basic building-block of government was the kafu, a community of anything from 1000 to 15,000 people living in or near a mud-walled town and ruled by a hereditary dynast called a fama." <br>"Sudanic societies were built on small agricultural villages or herding communities, sometimes but not always integrated into larger tribal and linguistic groups." <br>5.possibly another level below the fama, or someone who worked for the fama<br>2. Eastern Songhai (Songhai speakers) - rule based in Gao?<br>Wars of Askiya Muhammad Toure (1493-1528 CE), according to himself, "were undertaken to distract the Songhay-speaking element in his armies from meddling in the Mande-speaking western half of his empire where his own interests were strongest, and where he preferred to rule through slave armies recruited from his own war captives." <br>Askia Muhammad Toure divided the army into two parts: "one for the western provinces based in Timbuktu and one for the eastern provinces based in Gao." 3. Provinces<br>4. Village"Sudanic societies were built on small agricultural villages or herding communities, sometimes but not always integrated into larger tribal and linguistic groups." <br><br>2. ProvincesSonghai "was divided into provinces, cantons, villages, large cities of commercial character such as Djenne and Timbuktu, border areas which were strongholds such as Teghezza, Ualata, Nema, etc."<br>3. Cantons?4. Villages<br>2? Large cities: Djenne/Timbuktu3. koira-banda mundio"suburban administrator of a city."<br>2? Border strongholds: Teghezza/Ualata/Nema<br>'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 3674, 'temperature': 0.2})
