ription":  levels.<br>1. Emperor2. The Nine Courts<br>_Central government_<br>3. Secretariat (zhongshusheng)<br>3. Chancellery (menxiasheng)<br>3. Censorate (yushitai)<br>3. Three Dukes (sangong: Counsellor-in-chief chengxiang, Defender-in-chief taiwei, and Grand Preceptor taishi or taizai) later Eight Dukes (bagong: included the Grand Preceptor, Grand Mentor (taifu), the Grand Guardian (taibao), the Minister of Works (sikong), the Minister of Education (situ), and the Commander-in-chief (dasima) and the General-in-chief (dajiangjun)<br>3. Imperial Secretariat (shangshusheng)3-4. Royal Secretariat (shangshu tai) with six boards headed by presidents (shangshu)4-6. Six Ministries4-6. Board of Works (inferred vice minister and minister positions)7. Qibu (Bureau of Works)8. Lushi: "office manager in a general\'s headquarters, central government agency, or local administration"9. Clerk (inferred)<br>_Regional Government_<br>4. Regional Inspectors of Zhou<br>4. Regional Governors of Zhou<br>4. Regional Governors of Jun<br>4. Military Area Commanders (dudu or zongguan<br>4. Prince or Marquis5. Magistrate (ling) of a Xian (county)6. Lower-level officials (inferred)7. Clerks to the above offices (inferred)<br>The Nine Courts: "Regular court officials, arranged in courts (fu or si) helped to organize the imperial household affairs as chamberlains (qing)." The Nine Chamberlains (jiuqing), which were "Nine central government leaders and the agencies and their control ... began to be called the Nine Courts (jiusi)."<br>Secretariat (zhongshusheng): "the executive policy-formulating powers belonged to the Secretariat (zhongshusheng) that was the channel through which all memorials and documents flowed to the emperor and it was the agency that proposed and drafted all imperial rescipts, decrees (zhao) and edicts (ling)."<br>Chancellery (menxiasheng): "Policy consultants were gathered in an institution called Chancellery (menxiasheng) whose main function was to advise and to remonstrate."<br>Censorate (yushitai) headed by the Censor-in-chief: "The surveying agency of the officialdom was the Censorate (yushitai), headed by the Censor-in-chief (yushi dafu)."<br>"The highest posts or titles of the Jin central government were inherited from the Han." "Their staff was arranged in different sections (cao)."<br>Imperial Secretariat (shangshusheng): "The major institution of the Han central government, the Imperial Secretariat (shangshusheng), was ousted to a more routinely administrative role that controlled the Six Ministries (liubu, each headed by a minister shangshu and a vice-minister puye)."<br>Six Ministries (liubu, each headed by a minister shangshu and a vice-minister puye)<br>Western Jin "Bureau of Works, under the Board of Works."<br>Regional Government: "From the Three Kingdoms through the Western Jin, a three tier local government system, comprised of zhou (province), jun (region), and xian (county), was in place."<br>Regional inspectors: a zhou was a province "governed by regional governors zhoumu and controlled by regional inspectors, cishi"<br>"Each zhou had under its direct control a number of jun (regions). By the fall of the Western Jin, the zhou were reduced to de facto prefectures."<br>"19 provinces (zhou), 173 regions and fiefdoms (jun guo), and 2,459,840 households."<br>Jun (region) "administered by governors taishou." Jun were "eclipsed by the appearance of zhou (province)"<br>"Most regional governors were concurrently acting as military area commanders (dudu or zongguan)."<br>"Alongside with commanderies and districts there existed a lot of princedoms (wangguo) and marquisates (houguo), fiefs bestowed to members of the imperial house and, particularly the marquisates, to ministers of high merits."<br>"The administrative structure inherited Qin and Han reach down to the local level, the county (xian) with a population of several thousand or several tens of thousands..."'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 3065, 'temperature': 0.2})
