what we would consider public concerns."<br>_ Cairo line _<br>2<small>cairo</small> Magistracies. "The administration of Cairo and its inhabitants was in the hands of three traditional magistracies. The judges (qudah; sing. qadi) had a very broad jurisdiction that covered matters of civil law, and many urban problems were addressed in their courts.3<small>cairo</small> Chief of the Sergeants of the watch. Top police prefect.4<small>cairo</small> "The police prefects (wulah; sing. wali) saw to public order and security. They were particularly responsible for making the rounds at night and therefore also of fighting fires."<br>Overseer of the market (muhtasib) <br>"The quarter served as an important basis of communal association and as an essential administrative unit."<br>_ Egyptian line _<br>2egypt "diwan (government bureau) of Salar" <br>3egypt Na\'ib, governor of a mamlaka, an administrative district <br>4egypt Governor of a small town<br>5egypt. Village head.<br>_ Syrian line _<br>2<small>syria</small> Syrian chief governor Viceroy?3<small>syria</small> Na\'ib, governor of a mamlaka, an administrative district e.g. the bureaucracy niyaba of Safed contained:4. katib al-sirr/katib al-insha (chief secretary who wrote governor\'s letters, read mail) 5. muwaqqi (who ratified the governor\'s letters) 6. katib al-dast 7. katib al-darj (minor correspondent) <br>nazir (overseer who was responsible for financial management, expenditure, salaries) <br>kashif (inspector of bridges, agricultural lands and irrigation canals) <br>muhtasib (market inspector) <br>nazir diwan al-jaysh (superintendant of fiefs) <br>nazir al-mal (financial controller) <br>4. governor also had a dawadar (personal assistant) often sent to the villages to represent the governor and an ustadar (private caretaker) <br>4<small>syria</small> wali al-wulat of a niyaba "The wali was a police officer in charge of keeping law and order in town. His rank: Amir of Ten. One should not confuse him with wali al-wul