part in the direction of affairs."<br>"Archaeological evidence indicates that by Aksumite times there had developed a partly urbanized stratified society consisting of monarchy, surrounding elite, \'middle class\', and peasant/slave class." "high-quality grave goods, have been interpreted as those of \'middle-class\' Aksumites ... It might be expected that such a class would include government officials, scribes ..."<br>_Court government_<br>2. Treasurer and Secretary"The hellenized Syrians, Aedesius and Frumentius, who had been made royal slaves, were later promoted, one to the office of wine-pourer, the other to the position of secretary and treasurer to the Aksum king."<br>3.Vassal tribute was either sent or taken by the king who visited "accompanied by a numerous retinue".<br>3. Lesser officialGovernment officials, scribes.<br>"Leading chiefs as well as civil servants managed the administration. Levies and tributes were collected from the provinces."<br>4. ScribesGovernment officials, scribes.<br>5.<br>3. Manager of a Mint <i>inferred</i><br>4. Mint workerCoiners.<br>_Regional government_<br>2. Vassal king (negus)Aksumite term for ruler was \'negus\', and "Each \'people\', kingdom, principality, city and tribe had its own negus. Mention is made of army neguses ..."<br>"Control was established over a number of vassal states that sent tributes to the king." <i>This control was presumably fully established in the subsequent Aksum period.</i><br>Challenge of the Aksum monarch e.g Ezana was to enforce the submission of the northern Ethiopian principalities. <i>i.e. control over principalities was lacking in this period.</i><br>"The king exercised direct power in the capital territory, and he delegated power to regional leaders in the provincial areas." <i>This reference probably refers to the next Aksum periods.</i><br>"The state was divided into Aksum proper and its vassal kingdoms the rulers of which were subjects of the Aksum king of kings, to whom they paid tribute."<br>3. Vassal of a vassalSome vassal kings had their own vassals e.g. those in southern Arabia and Upper Nubia.<br>3. Negus of a cityAksumite term for ruler was \'negus\', and "Each \'people\', kingdom, principality, city and tribe had its own negus. Mention is made of army neguses ..."<br>4. Negus of a tribeThe neguses of the four tribes of Bega (Beja) ruled over about 1100 people, Agbo principality about 1000-1500.<br>'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2745, 'temperature': 0.2})
