eign (troops)and admiral of royal fleet. <br>2. Manager of the Antechamber (Psamtik I - Amasis). In charge of organizing royal audiences.3. Accountant scribes. According to the Petition of Peteise "he has accountant scribes to perform investigations throughout the country."<br>2. Viziers played a role of "supreme judge" <br>2. High Council (Psamtik I) (High Council reported directly to the king )"Convened to assist the sovereign in taking decisions" (Psamtik I)  The statuette of General Djedptahiufankh says the king "relies on his words on the day of the High Council ... distinguished by the king because of his excellent ideas ... pronouncing wise judgements in the Council of Nobles ... and speaking to them next to the king so that they were satisfied by his remarks."<br>3. Manager of the scribes of the council  Manages the audit office of the Royal Household (from second half of Saite Period). 4. Scribes of the council 5. Royal accounting scribes "dispersed throughout the various royal domains." <br>3. Manager of the royal boats - logistics within royal domain. (from Psamtik II) <br>3. Manager of the fields - "protecting the royal lands and their products from attempts at seizure." (from Psamtik II) <br>3. Senti - top administrator in charge of sacred domains. (from Psamtik II) <br>2. Council of Nobles (Psamtik I) (Council of Nobles also likely reported directly to the king. )"a deliberative meeting in which the king had to defend his point of view and obtain adherence." (Psamtik I)<br>3. Head/controller of the \'h. (Statue of Psmtk-snb)<br>3. pr pr-\'3 (The petition of P3-di-3st. Amasis.)<br>3. pr-nswt (Fragment of an Isis-statue with Horus of Nht-Hrw-hb)<br>3. Overseer of the \'hnwty (Statue of Psmtk)<br>3. Overseer of the treasury of the gold of the hnw. (Tomb of Hk3-m-s3.f. Amasis.)<br>_ Provincial line_ <i>ET: this is not in the correct order - nomarchs, counts, governors should not be level 4?</i><br>3. Thebaid regionReligious and political center - through a priestess based at the Amun temple. <br>3. Royal DomainLand, quarries, fisheries, flocks, ships, other assets. <br>Manager of the royal boats, Manager of the two granaries, Manager of the scribes of the High Camp. <br>Manager of the scribes of the council4. Scribes of the council5. Royal accounting scribes "dispersed throughout the various royal domains." <br>4. senti appointed to manage temple affairs (end of Saite Period)<br>4. Governors. e.g. Governor of Heracleopolis. <br>4. Counts<br>4. Principalities in DeltaHigh Chief of the Ma disappeared in 660s BCE. <br>Ruled by Libyan warlords. "Great Chiefs of the Ma." <br>4. Southern LandInitially administered as single unit (Psamtik I) ignoring nome boundaries. <br>"Southern Land" (Syene/Aswan to Memphis) under official called Leader of the Fleet (Psamtik I) based in Heracleopolis. He had financial duties as "revenue accountant for Middle and Upper Egypt." Title of Leader of Fleet probably no longer present c590s BCE. At least one holders known to have had title of governor. <br>By 592-591 BCE there was a "Governor of Heracleopolis" and a "traditional division in nomes ruled from a capital city under the authority of a governor."5. Nome ruler from c591 BCE<br>4. NomarchsAmasis "modified the role of nomarchs for the entire administration of Egypt." <br>5. Village levelSoldier rewarded "with a gold bracelet and an Egyptian village." <br>"In sum, the first Saite Period, the seventh century, was a reign of skillful politics aimed at taking over the territory. The second period, the sixth century (the years 592-591 could well mark this turning point), was an age of administrative standardization, and P. Rylands 9 reveals a country divided into nomes (ts.w)" e.g. Oxyrhynchos, Hermopolis, Cynopolis. "even if the territorial powers of intermediate level between the nome and the city, like the \'district-q\'h.t\', continue to be problematic."<br>A "superintendent of the central treasury" and a "chief steward" are known under Apries and Amasis.<br>'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 3704, 'temperature': 0.2})
